  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">COVAW: Tupo Tusikizwe
In this phase, COVAW project is carrying forward all the gains made so far. The project activities will focuson on movement building as part of the sustainability plan and on advocacy for prioritization of the needs of girls and women, including girls and women with disabilities in Kwale County. These activities include- advocacy meeting with Kwale County Government Executive to discuss and present the needs of women and girls in Kwale County. The key ask will be the county government to set aside funds for the establishment of the recovery centers, and engagement in public participation- 5 forums through which rights holders will be supported to participate in these platforms to lobby for the prioritization of their needs in the county plans and budgeting processes. The bedrock of these activities will be the skills and knowledge gained by COVAW through the accompaniment provided by theCoady Institute in the previous phases. Their awareness-raising activities will focus on citizen-led accountability (CLA) to empower rightsholders to demand accountability and lead efforts towards inclusion of their needs in county budget-making processes. The movement-building activities will partly focus on citizen-led accountability and they will seek out CBO representatives at training oftrainers following which they will expected to forward the learnings on CLA to empower the rightsholders they work with</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KENYAN PARAPLEGIC ORGANIZATION</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kenyan Paraplegic Organization (KPO) was registered 2004 as a non-governmental organisation with a vision of an inclusive society where all persons with disabilities enjoy equal rights, lead dignified and sustainable lives, and achieve their full potential.  KPO exists to inspire, empower and enhance quality of life of people with disabilities, particularly those with spinal injury (and related disabilities) to be productive members of the society.

KPO endeavours to achieve this through using participatory integratedapproaches that strengthen the capacity of community-based organisation structures to facilitate their participation in socio-economic and political processes. As an organisation, KPO has five strategic pillars:
- Governance and Accountability: to enable the participation of people with disabilities (PWDs), in civic education, lobby and advocacy, social audits, and use of media, to influence and enhance service delivery and accountability from duty bearers.
- Economic Empowerment: by promoting entrepreneurship and business development among PWDs, by enhancing access to micro-finance institutions and devolved funds for capacity building and credit facilities, value chain enhancement, entrepreneurship development, promotion of aggregation and collection centres, skills in resource mobilization.
- Psychosocial Support: through peer sessions, support with aiding devices, life skills development, social inclusion and integration, mentorship and exchange programmes, KPO contributes to reduce stigma against PWDs and increase their self-reliance.
- Rehabilitation; KPO is establishing a trauma and rehabilitation centre for care and support with long term personalized follow up for adherence, integration and social support.
- Organizational Development. KPO is a learning, self-reflecting Organization to continually enhance skills and knowledge in advocacy, resource mobilization, documentation, networking and socio-economic development strategies as well as continually improving on internalsystems and procedures.

KPO reaches PWDs in 9 Counties in Kenya: Nairobi, Kiambu, Laikipia, Kajiado, Nakuru, Baringo, Bomet, Bungoma and Busia. KPO has membership in various coalitions and Disability Persons Organisations (DPOs) that move forward the disability inclusion and diversity agenda.  KPO is uniquely placed as its constituent account for the highest number of persons with physical disability who again are the largest cohort in persons living with disability. KPO is also able to address cross-cutting challenges of all persons with disability while paying special attention to the uniqueness of its core constituent; this is also possible though effective linkages, networking and collaboration with other DPOs and partners.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
